Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["FindInfraParallel", "ObjectName",
 CellID->1224892054,ExpressionUUID->"8393d601-4014-44ba-afc9-60862df940ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"0452997e-3c65-4297-b27c-71ddc0fa6a52"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["FindInfraParallel"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/FindInfraParallel", 
     "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["line", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["p", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1db5f3d4-7e23-4d35-9535-38c619055500"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "gives the lines through ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " parallel to ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 ", as unary ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraLine"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraLine", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3f5b84d0-c536-4ae8-a493-993de16a49c1"],
 " wrappers.",
 "\n",
 "A count ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 " gives exactly ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "cdc89547-716c-4c77-83c1-bfc23526fec8"],
 "; ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"UpTo", "[", "n", "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6df11aa6-b294-4b99-abe3-a25414b5ad7f"],
 " up to ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 "; ",
 Cell[BoxData["All"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "eacf8f70-6626-424f-a63d-e0e4d304e6fd"],
 " all of them."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"d74993cd-13e9-4f52-adc4-87bcab68b44b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Definition: a parallel to ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 " through ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " is a ",
 StyleBox["maximal geodesic",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " through ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " every vertex of which is at the same distance from ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"35d6e0c9-c6e9-4ec7-bac6-ef9dd4de2b38"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This is Euclid's fifth postulate read intrinsically, and it is the \
strictest of the Euclidean constructions here. Two conditions must hold at \
once. The candidate must be a maximal geodesic, and it must lie in a single \
level set of the distance-to-",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 " function."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"562c5cc7-1137-4126-bada-94b5c5bd13a6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On a lattice that is hard to satisfy, and the examples below find ",
 StyleBox["none",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". The reason is the interaction of the two conditions. A maximal geodesic \
on a square grid is generally a staircase, and the distance from a staircase \
to a fixed line varies along it. A curve that does keep constant distance is \
usually not a geodesic. So the two requirements pull against each other."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"43737d65-6693-48c0-8d1c-99e750d67780"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This is worth stating carefully. Euclid's fifth postulate fails here, but \
not in the way it fails in hyperbolic geometry, where a point off a line has ",
 StyleBox["many", "TI"],
 " parallels. On these substrates it has ",
 StyleBox["none",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Non-existence, not multiplicity, is the failure mode."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"321fc362-2289-49cc-ad2d-ca292a30fceb"],

Cell["\<\
The count is zero on a bounded patch and also on a boundary-free flat torus, \
so it is not an artefact of the patch having a rim.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"4e4c5f49-8443-49b6-bdbb-16675218c528"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraParallelQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraParallelQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b33e125e-0dc9-4163-a671-5c25783ab611"],
 " asks a different question, and the two do not match. The predicate tests \
that two vertex sets are disjoint and at constant distance, and does not \
require either to be a maximal geodesic. So it accepts pairs this function \
would never return \[LongDash] concentric shells, for instance, are parallel \
by that test. A parallel can exist as a ",
 StyleBox["set", "TI"],
 " while no parallel exists as a ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1cac17c1-3b6a-4bd8-bfd7-8bbc62f1a1a9"],

